Deploy your portfolio to Hostinger VPS using Docker
Since your portfolio uses HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, we can package the entire website into a Docker image and deploy it on your Hostinger VPS using Nginx.
You won't need Apache, PHP, MySQL, or Node.js to run this portfolio.
1. Understand how the deployment works

Three concepts are important:
- Dockerfile: Instructions telling Docker how to build your website image.
- Docker image: A packaged version of your website and Nginx.
- Docker container: A running instance of that image on your VPS.
We'll first build and test the image on your computer, then deploy it to Hostinger.
2. Prepare your project folder
Extract your downloaded portfolio ZIP into a folder such as:
daniel-portfolio/
├── index.html
├── resume.html
├── README.txt
├── Dockerfile
├── nginx.conf
├── compose.yaml
└── .dockerignore


The last four files are the deployment configuration files we'll create.
3. Create your Dockerfile
In VS Code, open your portfolio folder and create a file named Dockerfile, with no extension.

FROM nginx:stable-alpine

COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf

COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
COPY resume.html /usr/share/nginx/html/resume.html

EXPOSE 80



What each instruction does:
Instruction	Purpose
FROM	Uses an existing lightweight Nginx image
COPY nginx.conf	Configures the web server
COPY index.html	Adds your homepage
COPY resume.html	Adds your resume page
EXPOSE 80	Documents the HTTP port Nginx listens on
Nginx is responsible for serving your HTML pages to visitors.
4. Create the Nginx configuration
Create nginx.conf in the same folder.

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name _;

    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index index.html;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }

    location /health {
        access_log off;
        return 200 "OK\n";
        add_header Content-Type text/plain;
    }
}



This configuration serves your website and provides a simple /health endpoint to check whether Nginx is responding.
5. Build the Docker image
Install Docker Desktop on your Windows computer if you haven't already, and make sure Docker Engine is running.
Open PowerShell in your portfolio folder:
cd "C:\path\to\daniel-portfolio"


Build the image:
docker build -t daniel-portfolio:1.0 .


Check that Docker created it:
docker images


You should see an image named daniel-portfolio with the tag 1.0.
6. Test the image locally
Start your container:
docker run -d `
  --name daniel-portfolio `
  -p 8080:80 `
  daniel-portfolio:1.0


The -p 8080:80 option maps port 8080 on your computer to port 80 inside the container.
Open:
http://localhost:8080
You should see your portfolio.
To inspect the container:
docker ps
docker logs daniel-portfolio


To stop and remove the test container:
docker stop daniel-portfolio
docker rm daniel-portfolio


7. Create Docker Compose configuration
Docker Compose makes it easier to deploy, restart, and update your portfolio.
Create compose.yaml:

services:
  portfolio:
    build: .
    image: daniel-portfolio:1.0
    container_name: daniel-portfolio
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "127.0.0.1:8081:80"



I chose port 8081 because you already have a hosted application. This helps avoid conflicts with existing services.
The 127.0.0.1 binding means only software running on the VPS can access this container directly. Later, a reverse proxy will make the portfolio publicly accessible over HTTPS.
You can test this configuration locally with:
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose ps


8. Connect to your Hostinger VPS
Open your Hostinger hPanel and find your VPS IP address.
In PowerShell:
ssh root@YOUR_VPS_IP


Replace YOUR_VPS_IP with the actual address. If Hostinger provided a different SSH username, use that instead.
Once connected, check your server:
cat /etc/os-release
docker --version
docker compose version
docker ps


Hostinger provides a Docker VPS template with Docker preinstalled. If you used that template, you may not need to install anything else. 

Hostinger



If Docker isn't installed, follow the official Docker installation guide for your VPS operating system.
Important: Before making changes, inspect the running containers using docker ps. Don't stop existing applications, reinstall the VPS operating system, or overwrite existing reverse-proxy settings.
9. Transfer your portfolio to the VPS
For your first deployment, I recommend transferring the source files and building the Docker image directly on the VPS.
From Windows PowerShell, outside your SSH session, navigate to the folder containing your portfolio:
cd "C:\path\to\daniel-portfolio"


Create a deployment directory on the VPS:
ssh root@YOUR_VPS_IP "mkdir -p /opt/daniel-portfolio"


Transfer your files:
scp index.html resume.html Dockerfile nginx.conf compose.yaml root@YOUR_VPS_IP:/opt/daniel-portfolio/


If you add CSS, JavaScript, or image folders later, transfer those too and update the Dockerfile to copy them into Nginx's HTML directory.
10. Build and start the container on Hostinger
Reconnect to the VPS:
ssh root@YOUR_VPS_IP


Navigate to the project:
cd /opt/daniel-portfolio


Build and deploy:
docker compose up -d --build


Check the result:
docker compose ps
docker compose logs --tail=50


Test from inside the VPS:
curl http://127.0.0.1:8081/health


Expected response:
OK


Your website is now running in Docker on the VPS, but it isn't publicly accessible yet.
11. Connect your domain and enable HTTPS
Because your VPS may already be hosting FileGuard, the safest approach is to use a separate domain or subdomain for your portfolio.
For example:
Application	Example address	Internal port
FileGuard	Your existing hosted URL	Existing configuration
Portfolio	portfolio.yourdomain.com	8081
These are examples, not DNS records that have been created.
The deployment should work like this:

To configure this:
1. Create a DNS A record pointing your portfolio subdomain to your VPS IP.
2. Identify whether your VPS already uses Nginx, Apache, Caddy, Traefik, or Nginx Proxy Manager.
3. Configure that existing reverse proxy to forward portfolio traffic to 127.0.0.1:8081.
4. Enable an HTTPS certificate for the portfolio domain.
5. Verify the website loads over HTTPS.
Hostinger also supports managing Docker projects through its Docker Manager. 

Hostinger



Avoid adding a second reverse proxy on ports 80 and 443 if another application is already using them.
12. Updating your website
Whenever you change your portfolio HTML:
1. Save the updated files on your computer.
2. Transfer them to /opt/daniel-portfolio.
3. Rebuild the image and recreate the container:
cd /opt/daniel-portfolio

docker compose up -d --build


View the logs:
docker compose logs --tail=50


Your updated portfolio will now be served from the rebuilt container.
Later, we can automate this process using GitHub so that approved changes to your portfolio repository trigger a deployment.

